## P5 producer investigation

- The old P5 probe passed in a local isolated run at `20261026T153132Z` on the
  CI source revision. The result was Java base/max 0/3 and Rust base/max 3/6.
- A likely cause is that the Broker's queryable maximum queue offset can lag
  Java's successful sends when the Rust probe starts. The remote TSV values
  were unavailable, so this remains an inference.
- Commit `92cae47d` passes Java's returned maximum offset into the Rust probe.
  Rust now polls `getMaxOffset` for up to 15 seconds before sending and reports
  the last observed offset on timeout. The Bash failure path prints both TSV
  outputs to preserve diagnostics if a mismatch recurs.
- The clean focused P5 scenario passed on `92cae47d` at `20261026T153933Z`
  (`source_code_dirty=false`, 68 seconds). Java stored offsets 0, 1, 2; Rust
  stored 3, 4, 5; both one-way reply variants and end-transaction returned
  without responses. Docker cleanup completed.
- The manifest points to the clean focused evidence directory.

## Next CI window

- The general test timeout is raised from 90 to 120 minutes because release
  examples compilation remained active at the 90-minute limit.
- The Docker matrix remains at 360 minutes. A fresh push-triggered workflow is
  required to validate the offset polling, complete release examples, and
  continue the full live scenario inventory.
- MAN-01 through MAN-04 remain owner-input blocked. No plan files were changed;
